WEBVTT

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:06.311
<v Rohan>I'm late, I'm late! Wait. How late am I, exactly?

00:00:06.311 --> 00:00:08.268
<v Mishu>That's a question about time.

00:00:08.168 --> 00:00:13.197
<v Mishu>Time is the interval between two events, like the bell ringing and you running in.

00:00:13.097 --> 00:00:15.500
<v Mishu>Its SI unit is the second.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.751
<v Ishita>But long ago, nobody had a watch. How did they know the time?

00:00:19.751 --> 00:00:22.812
<v Mishu>They looked up! People watched the sun and the moon.

00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:28.613
<v Mishu>The day, the month and the year all come from natural cycles they saw again and again.

00:00:28.613 --> 00:00:30.811
<v Rohan>So the sky was the first clock!

00:00:30.811 --> 00:00:33.975
<v Mishu>Then came the early clocks. Guess what these are.

00:00:33.975 --> 00:00:37.161
<v Ishita>That one uses sand, like an egg timer?

00:00:37.161 --> 00:00:40.479
<v Mishu>Yes! A sand clock. This is a simple water clock.

00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:44.602
<v Mishu>And this one, a sundial, reads the time from the sun's shadow.

00:00:44.602 --> 00:00:47.484
<v Rohan>A sundial? Do we have any in India?

00:00:47.484 --> 00:00:48.763
<v Mishu>Giant ones!

00:00:48.663 --> 00:00:56.317
<v Mishu>Maharaja Jai Singh the Second of Jaipur built them, and they still stand in Jaipur, Delhi, Ujjain and Varanasi.

00:00:56.317 --> 00:00:59.363
<v Rohan>A sundial as big as a building!

00:00:59.363 --> 00:01:05.049
<v Mishu>Even taller than most! The biggest one, in Jaipur, is twenty-seven metres high.

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:10.568
<v Mishu>Its shadow moves about a millimetre every second, so you can watch time move!
